      "text": "First, we need to find the total score for Cyprian's three test scores. \n\nTo find the total score for Cyprian's three test scores, we can use the given mean score of 90. The mean score is calculated by dividing the total score by the number of test scores.\n\nLet \\( S_{Cyprian} \\) be the total score for Cyprian's three test scores. The mean score of Cyprian's scores is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{S_{Cyprian}}{3} = 90\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\n\\[\nS_{Cyprian} = 90 \\times 3 = 270\n\\]\n\nNext, we know the test scores for Cyprian are 85, 87, and 92. We will verify the total score:\n\n\\[\n85 + 87 + 92 = 264\n\\]\n\nThis means we have a discrepancy since \\( 264 \\neq 270 \\). Therefore, we need to re-evaluate our approach. Let's denote the total score for all six test scores as \\( S_{total} \\). We already know the sum of the scores of Cyprian's three test scores:\n\n\\[\nS_{Cyprian} = 85 + 87 + 92 = 264\n\\]\n\nThus, the combined score for all six test scores is:\n\n\\[\nS_{total} = 264 + S_{Margaret}\n\\]\n\nMargaret's scores are 93, 94, and 98. Let \\( S_{Margaret} \\) be the combined total of Margaret's scores:\n\n\\[\nS_{Margaret} = 93 + 94 + 98 = 285\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total score for all six test scores is:\n\n\\[\nS_{total} = 264 + 285 = 549\n\\]\n\nNow, we find Margaret's mean score by dividing the total score of all six test scores by 6:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Margaret's mean score} = \\frac{S_{total}}{6} = \\frac{549}{6} = 91.5\n\\]\n\nThus, Margaret's mean score is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{91.5}\n\\]",
